A monosubstituted benzene has fi ve adjacent hydrogens, ortho has four adjacent hydrogens, meta has three adjacent hydrogens and one lone hydrogen, and para has two pairs of two adjacent hydrogens. The adjacent hydrogen group has as many wagging vibrational phases as there are C—H bonds. The mode that gives rise to the most intense IR band is the in-phase C—H wagging vibrations, found in the mean regions of
equation_inline
7 5 0 ~ \mathrm { c m ^ { - 1 } }
text
for fi ve and four adjacent hydrogens,
equation_inline
7 8 2 ~ \mathrm { c m ^ { - 1 } }
text
for three adjacent hydrogens,
equation_inline
8 1 7 ~ \mathrm { c m ^ { - 1 } }
text
for two adjacent hydrogens, and about
equation_inline
8 6 0 ~ \mathrm { c m } ^ { - 1 }
text
for a lone hydrogen wag [4]. The overall regions are summarized in Table 17-3 [23, 24].
